---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Cube from '../components/Cube.astro';
import { DOC, AIRBYTE_CONNECTORS_URL, REPO_URL } from '../consts';

const title = 'Where it fits | MetricMine';
const description =
  'Airbyte at the door, dbt and DuckDB in the middle, MCP at the edge, Claude Code beside you. Where MetricMine fits in a stack, and what its patterns mean for your own data.';

// docs/demo.md: the five tools and what each answers.
const tools = [
  { t: 'list_fact_categories', w: 'which fact categories exist, with row counts, the typed table to query, each category’s subject in the words of the people who approved its contracts, and the registry keys' },
  { t: 'get_schema', w: 'the declared field manifest behind a schema key' },
  { t: 'get_context', w: 'the full compiled context behind a schema key, in two named parts: data (what the columns are) and expert_context (what people wrote about them), plus the contract citation' },
  { t: 'query', w: 'one row-capped SELECT; anything else refuses, naming the failed check' },
  { t: 'lookup_record', w: 'every place a content key resolves: registry, fact, dimension, or a derived identity' },
];

// docs/sources-explained.md, section 10: the pattern map.
const patterns = [
  { h: 'Rows you are tempted to drop', p: 'Cancellations, returns, voids, soft deletes: retain and flag, state the consequence for every aggregate in the usage text, and let the consumer filter. Conservation against bronze then stays auditable.' },
  { h: 'Duplicates', p: 'Decide whether they are capture artifacts or events, write the decision as a property on the contract with the measured rate, and enforce the residual grain with an error-severity rule.' },
  { h: 'A reference newer than your events', p: 'A customer master, a product catalog, a location table refreshed after the transactions: declare the vintage on both contracts, measure the join completeness, set the floor just under it, and keep unresolved rows with null attributes.' },
  { h: 'Codes shared across systems', p: 'Customer ids, SKUs, site codes: conform them in silver with one normalization rule declared once and cited by every carrier, and let the gate refuse a key that only one table carries.' },
  { h: 'A series with gaps', p: 'Sensor readings, hourly rates, daily balances: declare the coverage with its count, and let the join completeness, not a silent inner join, show the effect.' },
  { h: 'Measurements absent when not observed', p: 'A gust, a discount, a reason code: say that null means not reported, not zero, in the field’s meaning, because an agent will read it.' },
  { h: 'Units and time zones', p: 'Units in column names; one UTC key for joining; local time as reading columns; the conformed calendar does the alignment.' },
  { h: 'Detail mixed with roll-ups', p: 'Economies and regional aggregates, accounts and their parents, SKUs and bundles: flag the roll-ups by an explicit list, never a name pattern, and say in the usage text what double counts.' },
  { h: 'When to unify and when to stand alone', p: 'Unify in silver when two sources describe the same entity and a question will need both columns on one row. Leave a category alone when it shares nothing, and let the star’s calendar align it in shape only.' },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/where-it-fits/">
  <PageHero eyebrow="Where it fits" title="Airbyte at the door. dbt and DuckDB in the middle. MCP at the edge.">
    MetricMine is built from standard parts and sits between the tools you
    already run. This page says what each part does here, what is exercised
    today and what is not, and how the demo's patterns map to data of your
    own.
  </PageHero>

  <section>
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">The front door</p>
        <h2>Bronze lands through PyAirbyte.</h2>
        <p>
          Nothing in the pipeline knows the demo's sources by name. Bronze
          lands whatever the source list names, the profiler measures whatever
          the profiling targets name, and the engine emits a category for
          every mapping contract. Airbyte publishes a catalog of{' '}
          <a href={AIRBYTE_CONNECTORS_URL}>600+ connectors</a> by its own
          count; PyAirbyte is the Python library that runs Airbyte
          connectors, and it is how bronze lands here.
        </p>
        <p>
          What is exercised today: the file connector, seven files deep, with
          reader options declared per source. What is not: other connector
          types. The standing non-goal is one connector type, many files, and
          the site says so rather than implying more.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">The warehouse</p>
        <h2>DuckDB, on purpose.</h2>
        <p>
          DuckDB is the local warehouse because the whole pipeline is meant to
          run on one machine, keyless, with no cluster and no account. What
          lives on the machine is a specification, not a silo: the contracts
          and the compiled context are warehouse-agnostic files in git, and
          every gold model is regenerated from them, so the data file is
          disposable and the meaning is portable.
        </p>
        <p>
          dbt profiles carry the execution plane. A second adapter is an
          experiment in the project's lane, not a promise on this page. The
          transforms are plain dbt Core models with contracts enforced, so
          what a dbt engineer already knows applies without translation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The edge</p>
        <h2>MCP, read-only, five tools.</h2>
        <p>
          Serving is read-only three layers deep: the connection opens
          read-only, the query tool accepts exactly one SELECT, and every
          result is row-capped with an explicit truncated flag. The demo guide
          wires the server into Claude Desktop.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="data">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Tool</th>
              <th>What it answers</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              tools.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><code>{r.t}</code></td>
                  <td>{r.w}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mono-note">
        The posture, the statement gate, and the row caps:{' '}
        <a href={DOC.servingSpec}>docs/spec/serving.md</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Beside you</p>
        <h2>Claude Code does the judgment work with you.</h2>
        <p>
          Silver is human-owned SQL under contracts, because silver is where
          meaning is decided. That is the work Claude Code is good at when it
          starts grounded, and the repository ships the context to ground it.
          Gold is mechanical: the engine emits it the same way every time, and
          no model gets to improvise there.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cards">
        <article class="card">
          <Cube />
          <h3>Adding a source.</h3>
          <p>
            One file to fetch, four entries to declare, two contracts to
            write, one model, and a paste into the star contract. Everything
            else is generated by a command and reviewed. The walkthrough was
            validated by adding a source that is not in the demo to a fresh
            clone in under fifteen minutes, eight files by hand.
          </p>
          <span class="under"><a href={DOC.addingASource}>docs/adding-a-source.md</a></span>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <Cube />
          <h3>Adopting a model you already have.</h3>
          <p>
            The scan derives a queue from the tree and names the next command
            per item; the describe stance drafts a contract for an existing
            model; verify-grain measures a declared grain rather than trusting
            it; enforce-properties adds only what the contract implies.
          </p>
          <span class="under"><a href={DOC.adoption}>docs/adoption.md</a></span>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <Cube />
          <h3>Asking Oscar.</h3>
          <p>
            Oscar is the repository's resident guide, a read-only subagent. Ask
            it how the system works, where a task is done, what a rule means,
            or how to keep silver clean, and it answers with the file and the
            line. Ask for a review and it runs the nine-item checklist, from
            the all-or-nothing column rule to the never-weaken gate, and stops.
          </p>
          <span class="under"><a href={DOC.oscar}>.claude/agents/oscar.md</a></span>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Bring your own data</p>
        <h2>Every pattern in the demo maps to a situation you will recognize.</h2>
        <p>
          The demo sources were chosen because they carry these situations in
          miniature. Each one is a decision written into a contract, a measured
          completeness on a join, a floor in a rule, and a line in a gate.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cards">
        {
          patterns.map((r) => (
            <article class="card">
              <Cube />
              <h3>{r.h}</h3>
              <p>{r.p}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="mono-note">
        What not to do: no shared entity dimension in gold; no join completeness
        that was not measured; no rows dropped without a recorded decision; no
        unit or null semantics that live only in someone's head. The full map:{' '}
        <a href={DOC.sourcesExplained}>docs/sources-explained.md</a>, section 10.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Fits when</p>
        <h2>The shape of a good fit.</h2>
        <ul class="cubes">
          <li><Cube size={18} /><p>Your data arrives as files or extracts, and you want the modeling and the meaning settled on one machine before anything is promoted anywhere.</p></li>
          <li><Cube size={18} /><p>You want an assistant to answer over governed data without a warehouse credential in its hands.</p></li>
          <li><Cube size={18} /><p>You want AI to draft the boring parts of modeling and a person to sign every change, with the record in git.</p></li>
          <li><Cube size={18} /><p>You want the meaning of the data to travel as files a team shares, and the data itself to be reproducible from them.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Does not fit when</p>
        <h2>The standing non-goals.</h2>
        <ul class="cubes">
          <li><Cube size={18} /><p>You need streaming, multi-tenancy, authentication, billing, or a hosted UI. Those are non-goals, written into the README and enforced as constraints.</p></li>
          <li><Cube size={18} /><p>You need a connector type other than the file connector. Other connector types are a standing non-goal today, not a roadmap item.</p></li>
          <li><Cube size={18} /><p>You need a warehouse other than DuckDB today. A second adapter is an experiment in the project's lane, not a promise.</p></li>
          <li><Cube size={18} /><p>You need an autonomous multi-step agent. The two agents here propose once and stop.</p></li>
          <li><Cube size={18} /><p>You need performance figures or a service level. What scale means here is measured on stated machines and published with its environment, in the repository, never as a promise.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Next</p>
      <h2>Ten minutes to a served star.</h2>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/get-started/">Get started</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/demo/">See the demo</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href={REPO_URL}>View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .band {
      background: var(--mm-bg-raised);
      border-block: 1px solid var(--mm-border);
    }
  </style>
</Base>
